  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold airmass builds into the region this \nweekend. Freeze Warnings are in effect for tonight. Another \nround of frost is possible Sunday night into Monday morning.\n\nThe last of the rains have exited the forecast (land) areas.\nHigh clouds remain across eastern NJ and Delmarva through. A \ntight pressure gradient exists between the departing low and \nhigh pressure building into the Central and Southern Plains. \nNorth winds remain mostly 5 to 10 mph and will continue to\ndiminish overnight.\n\nTonight, temperatures drop into the 30s nearly throughout, with\nlows down to or below freezing for the southern Poconos, Lehigh\nValley, and northwest New Jersey. Maintained freeze warnings as\ninherited, but mixing from lingering wind may preclude the southeastern\nextent of them from verifying. Confidence not high enough to cancel,\nhowever.\n\nHigh temperatures on Sunday will then only be in the 50s for most\nof the region despite mostly sunny skies. While another night of\ntemperatures below freezing is not expected, conditions will once\nagain potentially favor the development of some frost, mainly confined\nto the Lehigh Valley, Poconos and northern New Jersey. Lows are expected\nto be several degrees warmer than Saturday night mainly in the mid\nto upper 30s. Additional headlines can't be ruled out for Sunday\nnight if frost potential is realized where the growing season \nremains active, though the growing season may be ended in these \nareas by the freeze tonight.\n\nTemperatures will then begin to slowly moderate Sunday night \ninto early next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Uncertainty persists regarding a Nor'easter \ndeveloping off the coast of the southern Mid-Atlantic that may \ncause unsettled conditions and hazardous marine impacts early \nnext week.\n\nThe base of a deep trough axis will pass through the region late\ntonight into Sunday. As it does so, the trough axis will then \ncutoff into an upper level low which will meander over the \nsouthern Mid- Atlantic coast into early next week. At the same \ntime, strong and expansive high pressure will be building over \nthe Ohio Valley. While confidence is increasing regarding a \nnor'easter developing off the North Carolina coast by Sunday\ninto Sunday night, it must be said there remains a vast amount \nof uncertainty with regards to potential impacts to our region \ninto next week. \n\nConsiderable differences amongst global/ensemble guidance exists \nwith regards to just how close/long this system will get/last in our \nregion. Relative to the previous forecast discussion, there has not \nbeen much change. The EC ensemble member spread is fairly tightly \nclustered through the early part of next week, and then begins to \ndiverge, but generally has the low tracking northward and then \nmeandering off the Mid-Atlantic coast into mid or even late week. \nThe GEFS members are similar until about mid-week when they begin to \ndiverge more substantially. All of this being said, it doesn't \nappear likely that there will be significant impacts inland from \nthis system, even though they can't be entirely ruled out at this \ntime. What is increasingly likely though is at least some impacts to \ncoastal communities with periods of gusty northeast winds, some \nshowers, and potentially some coastal flooding concerns, potentially \nfor multiple days next week. We will continue to monitor the \nprogress of this developing system.",
